# GRUB boot-menu password for the kiosk image.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS
# Everything else in this image -- masked TTYs, disabled VT switching, locked
# accounts, Chromium policy -- is enforced by software that GRUB starts. A
# user who can edit the boot entry can append `init=/bin/sh` and get a root
# shell before any of it runs. A GRUB password is what closes that door.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE PUTTING A HASH HERE
# ------------------------------------
# A hash in this file is compiled into the ISO. If you ship that ISO to more
# than one customer, every one of them gets the SAME boot-menu password:
#
#   * a leak at any single site compromises every deployment you have shipped;
#   * it cannot be rotated on machines already installed in the field; and
#   * this file is tracked in git, so the hash is permanent in history and
#     open to offline cracking by anyone who can read the repository.
#
# So for a product, leave this EMPTY and use one of the two routes below.
# This file is only the right place for a single organisation building its own
# image for its own lab.
#
# ROUTE 1 -- PER INSTALLATION (default, nothing to configure)
# The setup wizard's "Install to Hard Disk" step asks for a boot-menu password
# and derives the hash in the browser with WebCrypto, so the password itself
# never reaches the agent, a process argument, or any disk. Each site -- or each
# workstation -- ends up with its own. Nothing is baked into the ISO.
#
# ROUTE 2 -- PER CUSTOMER ISO (locks the live USB menu too)
# Pass the hash in the build environment instead of committing it:
#
#   grub-mkpasswd-pbkdf2 -c 200000
#   docker run --privileged --rm \
#     -e LABKIOSK_GRUB_PBKDF2="grub.pbkdf2.sha512.200000..." \
#     -v "$PWD/distro-builder/out:/build/out" \
#     ghcr.io/akbhoi/labkiosk-iso-builder
#
# HOW TO GENERATE A HASH (either route)
#   docker run --rm -it ghcr.io/akbhoi/labkiosk-iso-builder grub-mkpasswd-pbkdf2 -c 200000
# Enter the password twice; copy the value starting at `grub.pbkdf2.sha512.`.
# Keep the password itself with your other lab administration credentials --
# it cannot be recovered from the hash.
#
# WHATEVER YOU SET, THE MACHINE STILL BOOTS UNATTENDED. Every menu entry is
# marked --unrestricted, so the password is asked for only when someone presses
# `e` to edit an entry or `c` for the GRUB command line.
#
# Leaving this empty builds an image whose LIVE boot menu is editable. That is
# fine for a VM or a bench test, and it is the right default for a shipped
# product, because installed workstations get their password from Route 1.

PASSWORD_PBKDF2=
